---
/**
 * The brand's one graphic device: the logo's parallel stripes. An ink
 * diagonal, a gap, a green diagonal: redrawn full-bleed at page scale.
 * Used once per page, home only.
 *
 * Notes:
 *  - 38deg matches the logo mark's slash (Logo.astro's .slash drifts ~293px
 *    over a ~372px height), so the full-bleed version and the monogram read
 *    as the same device.
 *  - The two rules are px-thick and skewed, never sized in a percentage of
 *    the container. A percentage tracks viewport WIDTH while the hero's
 *    height barely moves, which flattens the diagonal on wide screens.
 *  - The tint wash is viewport-relative on purpose: it is a background that
 *    has to reach the screen edge, not a shape whose proportions matter.
 *  - Nothing here animates, and the bands hold no text, which keeps
 *    subpixel antialiasing off the diagonal edges.
 *  - Decorative, so the whole stack is aria-hidden.
 *  - Below the two-column breakpoint the diagonals would cut through the
 *    headline, so they are dropped rather than redrawn.
 */
---

<div class="stripes" aria-hidden="true">
  <div class="tint"></div>
  <div class="ink"></div>
  <div class="green"></div>
</div>

<style>
  /* A zero-width anchor marking the content edge, not the viewport edge.
     .hero is full-bleed but the code window beside these stripes sits in
     .measure, which stops growing and centres itself; anchoring to the
     viewport let the stripes slide away from that window on anything wider.
     The inset mirrors .measure's own padding-inline calc. The bands hang
     off this box, so it never has to be as wide as the graphic. */
  .stripes {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    inset-inline-end: max(0px, calc((100% - var(--measure)) / 2 + min(var(--gutter), 6vw)));
    inline-size: 0;
    overflow: visible;
  }

  /* Full height of the hero, skewed around the top-right corner, so the
     drift (tan(angle) * height) follows the hero's real height rather than
     a guessed overscan. */
  .stripes > * {
    position: absolute;
    inset-block: 0;
    transform: skewX(38deg);
    transform-origin: 100% 0;
  }

  /* Offsets run leftward from the anchor. The tint is the wash the two
     rules sit on, and its right edge has to clear a viewport edge that is
     an unknown distance away, so it is pushed out past it and widened to
     still reach back past the rules. .hero's overflow:hidden trims it. */
  .tint  { background: var(--bg-alt); inset-inline-end: -100vw; inline-size: calc(300px + 100vw); }
  .ink   { background: var(--text);   inset-inline-end: 120px; inline-size: 16px; }
  .green { background: var(--logo);   inset-inline-end: 152px; inline-size: 32px; }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .stripes { display: none; }
  }
</style>
